==Pool D==

===Wales===
Wales named an initial 30-man squad for the tournament in June 1999. The highlight of the selection was centre [[Jason Jones-Hughes]], who was born in Australia, but was believed to be qualified for Wales through his father. The [[Rugby Australia|Australian Rugby Union]] (ARU) disputed Jones-Hughes' selection because he had already played for the [[Australian Barbarians]], who were considered Australia's ''de facto'' second team.<ref>{{cite news |title=Australia oppose Jones-Hughes selection |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/rugby_union/380823.stm |website=BBC News |date=29 June 1999 |access-date=16 March 2024 }}</ref> This led to a protracted legal battle over Jones-Hughes' eligibility for Wales, in which the [[World Rugby|International Rugby Board]] initially ruled in Australia's favour;<ref>{{cite news |title=Jones-Hughes ineligible for Wales |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/sport/rugby_union/387218.stm |website=BBC News |date=6 July 1999 |access-date=16 March 2024 }}</ref> on appeal, Wales were allowed to select Jones-Hughes,<ref>{{cite news |title=Jones-Hughes cleared to play for Wales |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/sport/rugby_union/439533.stm |website=BBC News |date=6 September 1999 |access-date=16 March 2024 }}</ref> but the ARU refused to release him from his contract with [[New South Wales Rugby Union|New South Wales]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Wales face 'World Cup forfeit' |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/sport/rugby_union/446164.stm |website=BBC News |date=14 September 1999 |access-date=16 March 2024 }}</ref> Nevertheless, Jones-Hughes was ultimately allowed to join the Wales team and was named on the bench for the tournament opener against Argentina.<ref>{{cite news |title=Wales unchanged for World Cup opener |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/rugby_world_cup/teams/wales/458172.stm |website=BBC News |date=26 September 1999 |access-date=16 March 2024 }}</ref>

